From: Stamp-P4 maintainers <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] sdio: the configured bus clock survives esp_remove (#41)

After a re-probe of the SDIO function (unbind/bind of the function or of
the whole mmc host, which is how a dead C6 link is brought back) the bus
ran at the 20 MHz the mmc core negotiated, not at the module's
clockspeed (5 MHz on these boards):

  boot:    mmc1 400 kHz -> 20 MHz -> ES_SDIO_CLOCK_APPLY requested_mhz=5
  rebind:  mmc1 400 kHz -> 20 MHz    (no ES_SDIO_CLOCK_APPLY line)

esp_probe() applies the clock only if sdio_context.sdio_clk_mhz is set.
That field is written once, by esp_init_interface_layer() at module
load, and esp_remove() ends with a memset of the whole context.  The
adapter pointer wiped by the same memset is fetched again in
init_context(); the clock was simply lost.

Keep it across the memset.

---
 sdio/esp_sdio.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

--- a/sdio/esp_sdio.c
+++ b/sdio/esp_sdio.c
@@ -647,6 +647,7 @@
 {
 	struct esp_sdio_context *context;
 	uint8_t prio_q_idx = 0;
+	u32 clk_mhz;
 
 	context = sdio_get_drvdata(func);
 
@@ -682,7 +683,15 @@
 			context->func = NULL;
 			context->adapter->dev = NULL;
 		}
+		/*
+		 * The bus clock is a module parameter, handed over once in
+		 * esp_init_interface_layer().  It has to be there for the next
+		 * probe, or a re-probed card stays at the speed the mmc core
+		 * picked.
+		 */
+		clk_mhz = context->sdio_clk_mhz;
 		memset(context, 0, sizeof(struct esp_sdio_context));
+		context->sdio_clk_mhz = clk_mhz;
 	}
 	esp_dbg("ESP SDIO cleanup completed\n");
 }
